{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## The leading indicator this story asks for ALREADY EXISTS — and it started working today\n\nThis story's last AC reads:\n\n    Consider a leading indicator so the NEXT instance is not discovered by a blocked\n    contributor -- e.g. a warn-level report of files above a headroom threshold, distinct from\n    the existing hard-cap failure. The hard cap tells you after it is too late; nothing\n    currently tells you before.\n\n**Something does tell you before. It has been computed all along and was reporting a false zero.**\n\n`module_size_distribution` carries a `near_cap_count` field, driven by `code_health.size_cap`\nand `size_near_fraction` (default 0.1, i.e. within 10% of the cap). On merged main just now:\n\n    module_size_distribution: {count: 435, near_cap_count: 37, over_cap_count: 0, max_loc: 799}\n    oversized_module_count:   0\n\n**37 files are within 10% of the hard cap.** That is the headroom report, live, today.\n\n## Why nobody saw it\n\nTwo independent faults stacked, both fixed on 2026-08-05:\n1. scc / lizard / jscpd were never installed, so every structural metric read `unavailable`.\n2. Once scc was installed, bug c5b3-1b8a-08dd-40af meant the adapter omitted `--by-file` and\n   returned a CONFIDENT ZERO -- `{count: 0, near_cap_count: 0, max_loc: 0}` -- on a repo whose\n   largest file was 7011 lines.\n\nSo the indicator existed, was wired, and reported `near_cap_count: 0` for its entire life. Anyone\nwho looked would have concluded there was no headroom problem. That is worse than absence.\n\nThis is the same failure this session found repeatedly: the checking machinery reporting a\nreassuring number it could not have computed. Cross-referenced on epic 6117-fcb7-d680-4b2f.\n\n## What this changes for THIS story\n\nThe AC as written (\"consider a leading indicator\") is largely satisfied by what now works. The\nremaining question is narrower and cheaper: the number is computed but nothing SURFACES it. It\nappears only if someone runs `rebar metrics` and reads the field. So the residual work is:\n\n- Decide a threshold at which 37-near-cap becomes a warning someone actually sees (CI warn step,\n  or the periodic health run proposed in task 03ef-6fb5-158b-4abd, which would be a natural host).\n- Do NOT build a second indicator. Wire up the one that exists.\n\nThe sweep half of this story is unaffected -- 40 files over 700 LOC, and the near-cap population\nis now measurable at 37 rather than estimated.\n\n## Caveat\n\n`near_cap_count` depends on `code_health.size_cap` being set. It is set in `rebar.toml` (800) as\npart of c5b3's dogfooding. A client that does not set `size_cap` gets `near_cap_count: null` --\nso this indicator is only live for projects that configure the cap, which is worth stating\nwherever it gets surfaced."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785921761941320001,"uuid":"f7180a6b-cd0e-4d67-bb68-c5ea89af8759"}